<Ui>
    <!--
        The quest-timer window — the small dialog under the minimap that counts a timed quest down
        (decision 1150, B234). Geometry, art, layout and behaviour are QUOTED from the real 1.12
        QuestTimerFrame (extracted Interface\FrameXML\QuestTimerFrame.xml + .lua): a 160x72
        UI-DialogBox backdrop with the 256x64 header art and the QUEST_TIMERS title on it, twenty
        140x16 rows stacked flush from TOP -30, and a height of 45 + 16*n for n live timers.

        It is a LIST OF TIMES, not a bar. The report this closes guessed a mirror-timer-style bar,
        and the reference file settles it: one `SecondsToTime` line per timed quest ("14 Mins 20
        Secs"), clickable to open the quest log at that quest, hovering to name it. The mirror
        timers (MirrorTimer.xml, 0874) are a different mechanism entirely — those are server-pushed
        values with a rate to integrate, this reads the quest log's own deadlines.

        The feed is the engine's GetQuestTimers()/GetQuestIndexForTimer(), which subtract the
        server's wall clock (SMSG_QUERY_TIME_RESPONSE) from each quest-log slot's absolute deadline
        per call — the reference's own division of labour, and why the OnUpdate below can repaint a
        ticking number without the quest-log snapshot changing under it. No timed quests → the
        bindings return no values at all → the frame hides, which is the ref's own signal.

        Departures, each named:
        - BARE frame names (no `Benilla` prefix), like DurabilityFrame and GroupLootFrame1 before
          it (decision 0591 §3): UIParent_ManageFramePositions resolves its stack through a literal
          getglobal(name), and the ref's own Lua below reaches its rows by
          getglobal("QuestTimer"..i.."Text"). Prefixing would silently unseat the frame and break
          the row lookup at once.
        - Lua 5.0 `arg`/`arg.n` varargs written as select("#", ...)/select(i, ...), the 5.1-native
          spelling and this tree's house style (TalentFrame.xml / WorldMapFrame.xml's precedent).
        - The ref's XML anchor (TOPRIGHT of MinimapCluster +10,0) is transcribed and, like every
          managed HUD frame, DEAD ON ARRIVAL: UIParent_ManageFramePositions re-seats it at
          -CONTAINER_OFFSET_X on every pass. Kept verbatim anyway so the file reads as the ref's.
        - `toplevel="true"` is dropped, and the reason given here was half wrong: the raise's real
          triggers are **Show**, drag-start and mouse-down, not "raise-on-click for movable dialogs"
          (wow-re `ui/scratch/toplevel-raise.md`). The verb now exists (`script::object::toplevel`),
          so the standing reason is only the second one — this frame is a managed HUD strip that
          UIParent_ManageFramePositions re-seats and that overlaps nothing in its own stratum, so
          the occlusion gate would decline every raise anyway. Re-adding it is one word if that
          stops being true.
        - QuestTimerFrame_OnShow/OnHide call the manage pass, as the ref does — that is what lets
          the durability guy and the quest tracker drop below this window when a timer appears.
        - The ref's own commented-out QuestTimerFrame_UpdatePosition (its pre-manage-pass
          positioning) is left out rather than transcribed as dead code.

        NOT a departure, though it looks like one: the row buttons are laid out flush (0,0 between
        them) while each is 16 tall and the frame grows by exactly 16 per timer. That is the ref's
        own arithmetic (45 + 16*n against a 72-tall empty frame), not a rounding of ours.
    -->
    <Script><![CDATA[
        -- ref GlobalStrings.lua:3241 — the window's title, under its real key so the ref's own
        -- `text="QUEST_TIMERS"` below resolves through the loader's global-string lookup verbatim
        -- (loader/mod.rs `resolve_text`). Declared here because we load no GlobalStrings.lua;
        -- guarded, in case a later file ships one.
        QUEST_TIMERS = QUEST_TIMERS or "Quest Timers"
        -- ref QuestLogFrame.lua:2. Declared here too because the ref's hide loop below runs to it
        -- and this file may load before the quest log's own copy.
        MAX_QUESTS = MAX_QUESTS or 20

        -- ref QuestTimerFrame_OnLoad (QuestTimerFrame.lua:1-6).
        function QuestTimerFrame_OnLoad(frame)
            frame:RegisterEvent("QUEST_LOG_UPDATE")
            frame:RegisterEvent("PLAYER_ENTERING_WORLD")
            QuestTimerFrameNumTimers = 0
            QuestTimerFrameUpdating = nil
        end

        -- ref QuestTimerFrame_OnEvent (:8-14). The `updating` latch is the ref's own re-entrancy
        -- guard: QuestTimerFrame_Update's Show() fires OnShow → the manage pass, and a
        -- QUEST_LOG_UPDATE arriving inside that would re-enter this.
        function QuestTimerFrame_OnEvent()
            if not QuestTimerFrameUpdating then
                QuestTimerFrame_Update(GetQuestTimers())
            end
        end

        -- ref QuestTimerFrame_Update (:16-32), with select() for the 5.0 vararg table.
        function QuestTimerFrame_Update(...)
            QuestTimerFrameUpdating = 1
            local n = select("#", ...)
            for i = 1, n do
                -- `select(i, ...)` expands to EVERY value from i on, and in a call's last argument
                -- position they all get passed — so an inlined `SecondsToTime(select(i, ...))`
                -- hands timer i+1 to SecondsToTime's `noSeconds` and silently drops the seconds
                -- off every row but the last ("14 Mins" instead of "14 Mins 20 Secs"). The local
                -- takes the first value only. The ref's `arg[i]` had no such hazard; this is the
                -- 5.0→5.1 vararg swap's own trap, caught by the test that expected the seconds.
                local seconds = select(i, ...)
                getglobal("QuestTimer" .. i .. "Text"):SetText(SecondsToTime(seconds))
                getglobal("QuestTimer" .. i):Show()
            end
            for i = n + 1, MAX_QUESTS do
                getglobal("QuestTimer" .. i):Hide()
            end
            QuestTimerFrameNumTimers = n
            local frame = getglobal("QuestTimerFrame")
            if n > 0 then
                frame:SetHeight(45 + (16 * n))
                frame:Show()
            else
                frame:Hide()
            end
            QuestTimerFrameUpdating = nil
        end

        -- ref QuestTimerFrame_OnUpdate (:34-38): re-read every frame while any timer is live —
        -- that, not an event, is what makes the number tick. Costs nothing while hidden (the
        -- engine's OnUpdate gate is effective-visibility) and nothing while empty (numTimers 0).
        function QuestTimerFrame_OnUpdate()
            if QuestTimerFrameNumTimers > 0 then
                QuestTimerFrame_Update(GetQuestTimers())
            end
        end

        -- ref QuestTimerButton_OnClick (:40-44): open the log at that quest.
        function QuestTimerButton_OnClick(id)
            local index = GetQuestIndexForTimer(id)
            if not index then
                return
            end
            ShowUIPanel(getglobal("QuestLogFrame"))
            SelectQuestLogEntry(index)
            BenillaQuestLogFrame_Update()
        end
    ]]></Script>

    <Button name="QuestTimerTemplate" virtual="true">
        <Size>
            <AbsDimension x="140" y="16"/>
        </Size>
        <Layers>
            <Layer level="BACKGROUND">
                <FontString name="$parentText" inherits="GameFontHighlightSmall">
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="TOP"/>
                    </Anchors>
                </FontString>
            </Layer>
        </Layers>
        <Scripts>
            <!-- ref QuestTimerFrame.xml:18-27. The tooltip names the quest the row belongs to;
                 GetQuestLogTitle's FIRST return only (the rest of its 6-tuple is level/tag/state). -->
            <OnEnter>
                local index = GetQuestIndexForTimer(self:GetID())
                if index then
                    GameTooltip:SetOwner(self)
                    GameTooltip:SetText(GetQuestLogTitle(index), 1.0, 1.0, 1.0)
                    GameTooltip:Show()
                end
            </OnEnter>
            <OnLeave>GameTooltip:Hide()</OnLeave>
            <OnClick>QuestTimerButton_OnClick(self:GetID())</OnClick>
        </Scripts>
    </Button>

